WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:02.680
In front of us, we have another screenshot

00:02.680 --> 00:04.720
from Khan Academy and I've
modified it a little bit

00:04.720 --> 00:06.170
so I have a little bit of extra space.

00:06.170 --> 00:08.490
And it says, divide the polynomials.

00:08.490 --> 00:10.099
The form of your answer should either be

00:10.099 --> 00:12.176
a straight up polynomial or a polynomial

00:12.176 --> 00:15.610
plus the remainder over x minus five,

00:15.610 --> 00:17.934
which we have here in the denominator,

00:17.934 --> 00:20.010
where p of x is a polynomial
and k is an integer.

00:20.010 --> 00:21.310
So, we've done stuff like this,

00:21.310 --> 00:23.950
but like always, I encourage
you to pause this video

00:23.950 --> 00:25.460
and work on this on your own.

00:25.460 --> 00:27.485
And if you were doing
this on Khan Academy,

00:27.485 --> 00:29.080
there's a little bit of an input box here

00:29.080 --> 00:29.913
where you'd have to type in the answer,

00:29.913 --> 00:31.810
but let's just do it on paper for now.

00:32.740 --> 00:33.660
All right.

00:33.660 --> 00:34.900
So, we're trying to figure out

00:34.900 --> 00:39.490
what x minus five divided into

00:41.080 --> 00:43.460
two x to the third power.

00:43.460 --> 00:44.840
Actually, I want to be careful here

00:44.840 --> 00:48.270
because I want to be very, very organized

00:48.270 --> 00:50.630
about my different degree columns.

00:50.630 --> 00:52.440
So, this is my third degree column.

00:52.440 --> 00:54.550
And then I want my second degree column.

00:54.550 --> 00:56.670
But there is no second degree term here.

00:56.670 --> 00:57.730
There's a first degree term.

00:57.730 --> 00:59.080
So, I'll write it out here.

01:00.112 --> 01:00.945
So, minus 47x.

01:02.420 --> 01:03.720
And actually, to be even more careful

01:03.720 --> 01:06.930
I'll write plus zero x squared.

01:06.930 --> 01:09.226
And then I have minus 15.

01:09.226 --> 01:11.930
By putting that plus zero x squared,

01:11.930 --> 01:14.290
that's making sure I'm doing good,

01:14.290 --> 01:17.273
I guess degree place column hygiene.

01:17.273 --> 01:20.217
All right, so now we
can work through this.

01:20.217 --> 01:23.200
And first we could say hey, how many times

01:23.200 --> 01:25.550
does x go into the
highest degree term here?

01:25.550 --> 01:27.520
Well, x goes into two x to the third power

01:27.520 --> 01:29.880
two x squared times.

01:29.880 --> 01:32.730
We'd want to put that in
the second degree column.

01:32.730 --> 01:33.990
Two x squared.

01:33.990 --> 01:35.500
You can see how it would've gotten messy

01:35.500 --> 01:37.470
if I'd put negative 47x here.

01:37.470 --> 01:39.450
I'd be like where do I
put that two x squared

01:39.450 --> 01:41.870
and you might confused yourself,

01:41.870 --> 01:44.340
which none of us would want to happen.

01:44.340 --> 01:46.990
All right, two x squared
times negative five

01:46.990 --> 01:50.330
is negative 10x squared.

01:50.330 --> 01:54.039
Two x squared times x is
2x to the third power.

01:54.039 --> 01:56.310
Now we want to subtract
what we have in red

01:56.310 --> 01:57.830
from what we have in blue.

01:57.830 --> 02:00.390
So, I'll multiply them
both by negative one.

02:00.390 --> 02:01.580
So that becomes a negative

02:01.580 --> 02:03.740
and then that one becomes a positive.

02:03.740 --> 02:05.460
And that's actually one
of the biggest areas

02:05.460 --> 02:06.660
for careless errors.

02:06.660 --> 02:09.330
If you had negative here and
you just want to subtract it

02:09.330 --> 02:11.120
because you know you have to subtract it.

02:11.120 --> 02:12.859
Be like no, I'm subtracting a negative,

02:12.859 --> 02:13.790
it needs to be a positive now.

02:13.790 --> 02:14.950
All right.

02:14.950 --> 02:19.730
So, zero x squared plus
10x squared is 10x squared.

02:19.730 --> 02:21.610
And then the two x to the third

02:21.610 --> 02:24.000
minus two x to the third is just zero.

02:24.000 --> 02:27.273
And then we can bring
down that negative 47x.

02:29.330 --> 02:34.317
And once again, we look at
the highest degree terms.

02:34.317 --> 02:38.775
X goes into 10x squared 10x times.

02:38.775 --> 02:41.480
So, plus 10x.

02:41.480 --> 02:45.673
10x times negative five is negative 50x.

02:48.590 --> 02:52.270
10x times x is 10x squared.

02:52.270 --> 02:55.220
And once again, we want to
subtract what we have in teal

02:55.220 --> 02:56.600
from what we have in red.

02:56.600 --> 02:59.080
So we could multiply both
of these times negative one.

02:59.080 --> 03:00.480
That becomes a negative.

03:00.480 --> 03:02.373
This one becomes a positive.

03:03.240 --> 03:08.230
Now, negative 47x plus
50x is positive three x.

03:08.230 --> 03:12.000
And then 10x squared minus
10x squared gets canceled out.

03:12.000 --> 03:14.340
Bring down that 15.

03:14.340 --> 03:16.030
Come on down.

03:16.030 --> 03:18.810
Used to watch a lot of
Price is Right growing up.

03:18.810 --> 03:20.490
Never quite made it to the show.

03:20.490 --> 03:21.660
All right.

03:21.660 --> 03:25.260
X goes into three x how many times?

03:25.260 --> 03:27.550
It goes three times.

03:27.550 --> 03:30.940
Three times negative five is negative 15.

03:30.940 --> 03:33.360
Three times x is three x.

03:33.360 --> 03:36.320
We want to subtract the
orange from the teal.

03:36.320 --> 03:40.290
And so, this becomes a negative,
this becomes a positive.

03:40.290 --> 03:43.610
Negative 15 plus 15 is zero.

03:43.610 --> 03:46.213
And three x minus three x is zero.

03:46.213 --> 03:47.594
So, you're just left with zero.

03:47.594 --> 03:48.813
So, no remainder.

03:48.813 --> 03:53.100
So this whole thing, you could
re-express or simplify as

03:53.100 --> 03:57.100
two x squared plus 10x plus three.

03:57.100 --> 03:58.470
And once again, if this
was on Khan Academy,

03:58.470 --> 03:59.900
there would be a little
bit of an input box

03:59.900 --> 04:01.682
that looks something like this

04:01.682 --> 04:03.500
and you would have to type this in.

04:03.500 --> 04:06.580
Now, if you wanted these to
be exactly the same expression

04:06.580 --> 04:08.690
you would also need to
constrain the domain.

04:08.690 --> 04:12.860
You would say, okay four x
does not equal positive five.

04:12.860 --> 04:14.190
And the reason why you
have to constrain that

04:14.190 --> 04:18.300
is the whole reason why we can
even divide by x minus five

04:18.300 --> 04:21.510
is we're assuming that x minus
five is no equal to zero.

04:21.510 --> 04:23.280
And it's generally not equal to zero

04:23.280 --> 04:26.410
as long as x does not
equal to positive five.

04:26.410 --> 04:28.120
But for the sake of that exercise,

04:28.120 --> 04:30.863
you don't need to constrain
the domain like this.
